  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is sufficient for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{it is sufficient that}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is necessary for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option IV reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{it is necessary that}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and IV only.\"",
